Tell a hosted VST3 the display scale before attaching its view.

JUCE 8.0.14 learns the platform scale factor only through
NativeScaleFactorNotifier, whose callback hops through MessageManager::callAsync
before it writes VST3PluginWindow::nativeScaleFactor. That hop cannot complete
until the editor's constructor and its first componentVisibilityChanged() have
returned, so view->attached() - which is what makes a plug-in build its UI -
always runs while nativeScaleFactor is still the 1.0 placeholder, and the
setContentScaleFactor() calls before it report an unscaled display. The real
factor is pushed a message loop later.

A plug-in that re-reads the host's scale whenever it changes recovers, and most
current ones do. DPF, iPlug2 and other older frameworks read it once, while
constructing the UI, so they never see anything but the 1.0 and draw at 1:1 for
the life of the window - a little over half size on a 175% display, next to a
host UI that is scaled correctly.

The peer already knows the scale by the time we attach, so ask it directly
instead of waiting to be told. Sizing is untouched: componentVisibilityChanged()
already calls resizeToFit() straight after attaching, which re-reads the view's
size under the corrected scale. macOS is excluded because nothing is reported to
a plug-in there at all - see juce-vst3-no-mac-content-scale.patch.

diff --git a/modules/juce_audio_processors/format_types/juce_VST3PluginFormat.cpp b/modules/juce_audio_processors/format_types/juce_VST3PluginFormat.cpp
--- a/modules/juce_audio_processors/format_types/juce_VST3PluginFormat.cpp
+++ b/modules/juce_audio_processors/format_types/juce_VST3PluginFormat.cpp
@@ -457,10 +457,52 @@ private:
                       jmax (10, logicalSize.getHeight()));
     }

+   #if ! JUCE_MAC
+    /*  PLECTRIFY_SCALE_BEFORE_ATTACH: take the display scale straight from the
+        peer, for the sake of plug-ins that only ever read it once.
+
+        nativeScaleFactor is otherwise written by NativeScaleFactorNotifier
+        alone, which reports through MessageManager::callAsync and so cannot
+        land until after the constructor and the first
+        componentVisibilityChanged() have run to completion. view->attached()
+        therefore always fires while nativeScaleFactor still holds its 1.0
+        placeholder, and the plug-in builds its editor believing the display is
+        unscaled; the true factor arrives a message loop later. A plug-in that
+        re-reads the host's scale on every change catches up - most modern ones
+        do - but DPF, iPlug2 and other older frameworks apply it only while
+        constructing their UI, so they stay at 1:1 for the life of the window
+        and draw an editor a little over half size on a 175% display.
+
+        The peer already knows the answer here, so ask it rather than wait to be
+        told. Sizing is left alone: componentVisibilityChanged() calls
+        resizeToFit() immediately after attaching, which re-reads the view's
+        size under the corrected scale.
+    */
+    void adoptPeerScaleFactor()
+    {
+        if (auto* peer = getTopLevelComponent()->getPeer())
+        {
+            const auto platformScale = (float) peer->getPlatformScaleFactor();
+
+            if (! approximatelyEqual (platformScale, nativeScaleFactor))
+            {
+                nativeScaleFactor = platformScale;
+                setContentScaleFactor();
+            }
+        }
+    }
+   #endif
+
     void attachPluginWindow()
     {
         if (pluginHandle == HandleFormat{})
         {
+           #if ! JUCE_MAC
+            // PLECTRIFY_SCALE_BEFORE_ATTACH: the plug-in has to know the display
+            // scale before it builds its UI - see adoptPeerScaleFactor().
+            adoptPeerScaleFactor();
+           #endif
+
             #if JUCE_WINDOWS
              pluginHandle = static_cast<HWND> (embeddedComponent.getHWND());
             #endif
